# Plan Readiness Gates — 3-Tier Model

SSOT: `unified-trading-pm/plans/PLAN_FORMAT.md`

## RULE: Required YAML Fields

Every `.md.md` file in `plans/active/` MUST include these fields in YAML frontmatter:
- `type`: one of `code | infra | deployment | business | mixed`
- `epic`: one of `epic-code-completion | epic-deployment | epic-business | epic-infra | none`
- `completion_gates`: with `code`, `deployment`, and `business` subkeys
- `repo_gates`: list of repos this plan touches with their current gate level

Missing these fields is a plan format violation — fix before committing.

## RULE: ai/ vs active/ Split

- AI-generated plans → `plans/ai/` ONLY (never directly to `plans/active/`)
- User-approved plans → `plans/active/` (must have full YAML format)
- On promotion: check INDEX.md for slug conflicts; resolve before moving

## RULE: Archive Eligibility

A plan is eligible for archive ONLY when ALL repos in `repo_gates` have reached the level
declared in `completion_gates`. Partial repo coverage is NOT sufficient.

| Plan type | Minimum gate to archive |
|-----------|------------------------|
| `code` | C5 (quickmerge) for all repos |
| `infra` / `deployment` | D3 (staging integration) for all repos — required even during code-completion epic |
| `business` | B6 (user approved) + domain B3 KPIs met |
| `mixed` | Highest required gate across declared types |

NEVER mark a plan done/archived because most todos are complete while some repos are still at C0–C4.

## RULE: Gate Definitions

### Code (C)
- C0 = not started
- C1 = implementation complete, not tested
- C2 = unit tests passing, coverage maintained
- C3 = linter + codex gates (ruff, basedpyright)
- C4 = full quality-gates.sh Pass 1
- C5 = quickmerge complete (merged to staging/main)

### Deployment (D)
- D1 = deployable (infra, configs, Docker)
- D2 = CI/GHA smoke tests pass — emulators/mocks, NO real service calls
- D3 = staging integration tests pass — REAL service/API calls (SIT suite)
- D4 = load/performance tests pass in staging
- D5 = production-ready (security + operational gates green)

### Business (B)
- B1 = acceptance criteria defined
- B2 = scenario analysis complete
- B3 = performance targets met (ML accuracy, P&L, execution alpha, latency — declared per plan)
- B4 = batch vs live validation (t+1 check)
- B5 = staging vs live parity (N-minute replay)
- B6 = user approved

## RULE: blocked_by in Todos

When a plan is blocked by another plan's incomplete work, use `blocked_by: <plan-slug>` on the
specific todo item — not on the completion_gates. The gates represent what this plan owns;
external dependencies belong in todos.

## RULE: New Plans from AI Sessions

When any agent (Claude Code, Cursor, GHA autonomous) generates a new plan:
1. Write it to `plans/ai/` with proposed frontmatter
2. Do NOT write it to `plans/active/`
3. Include a comment at the top: `# AI-GENERATED — awaiting user review and promotion`
4. When the user promotes it, run the conflict check and add to INDEX.md

## WHY This Matters

Plans were previously archived when most todos were done, ignoring partial repo coverage and
infrastructure gaps owned by separate plans. A plan that "implements X" but skips 3 repos is
not done. A plan that needs deployment infra that doesn't exist yet isn't blocked — it just
needs the right gates declared. This model makes blocking relationships explicit.

